EXT. PETTING ZOO - BIRTHDAY PARTY - DAY

Hay bales, a plywood barn, a paddock of goats in party hats. Balloons bob from the fence. HETTIE LOCKWOOD, eight, stands on a stool at the picnic table, chin on her fist, watching a goat eat a paper plate, while her father BOYD LOCKWOOD, forties, hurries from the cake table with a plastic knife and a stack of napkins, calling out to nobody that the candles need relighting, and trips over a feed bucket, and recovers, and laughs at himself a little too loudly.

HETTIE
Dad, he ate the plate.

BOYD
He's a goat, honey. It's what they do.

HETTIE
Mom said no one could come who we didn't invite.

BOYD
Mom isn't here.

He scrapes the cake into a pile and starts sticking candles in it. Hettie looks past him, toward the gate, and goes very still.

A STRANGER in a rumpled linen suit and sunglasses stands at the entrance with a gift bag, scanning the crowd. He lifts the glasses. Waves at Hettie like he's known her all her life.

STRANGER
Hey, kiddo!

BOYD
(turning)
Can I help you?

STRANGER
It's Felix. Uncle Felix. Your brother, Boyd. Hello?

Boyd stops with a candle in his fist. The goat bleats. Hettie hops off the stool and runs toward the gate, and Boyd, watching her go, drops the candle into the cake.

BOYD
Felix.

FELIX
I brought a present.

Hettie hugs his leg. Boyd stares at his brother's face for a long time, the whole party going quiet behind him, and then Boyd laughs again, but this time it cracks halfway.

BOYD
You son of a-

HETTIE
Dad! Language!
